  "query_preview": "/THURSDAY THROUGH MONDAY/...\nIssued at 323 AM CDT Tue Oct 20 2026\n\nUpper ridging will remain situated over the Plains Thursday into \nFriday, with upper troughing coming onshore over California and \ntraversing into the Rocky Mountain during this time frame as well. \nWith high pressure settling over our region, daytime highs will be \nwell above climatological norms (15 to 20 degrees), with highs being \nin the mid to upper 70s across western and north central Nebraska. \nFriday afternoon in particular will be the warmest for the week. \n25th/75th percentile for daytime highs are 71 to 75 respectively, \nwith southwest Nebraska having at least a 30 - 50 percent chance of \nbreaking 80 degrees. Daytime mixing will bring strong southerly \nwinds, 20 to 30 mph, gusting up to 45 mph across the region. NBM \nprobabilities, while typically on the higher end of this range, \nstill have gusts in excess of 45 mph at 30 to 50 percent. \n\nAs the upper trough crosses the Rockies off to our west, a cold \nfront will arrive late Friday night into our area. There still is \nsome model disagreement on how fast the front progresses \nsoutheastward across the region, with the GFS pushing the front \nmostly out of Nebraska by Saturday evening, while the Euro has it \nmoving more slowly and stalling out in southern Nebraska. \nNevertheless, this front will be the catalyst for cooler, seasonable \nhighs in the 60s as well as increasing chances for showers and \nperhaps a few thunderstorms. QPF totals will remain fairly low with \nareas of southwest Nebraska to the central Sandhills potentially \nreceiving a tenth of an inch or less. \n\nBetter precipitation chances could come Sunday through Tuesday \nmorning, when the upper low stalls out over the Four Corners region. \nThis will create southwest flow aloft, bringing a stream of Gulf \nmoisture up into our region. Latest guidance suggests increased \nchances for primarily rain showers arriving to southwest Nebraska \nearly Sunday morning and spreading northeastward across north \ncentral Nebraska throughout the day and into Monday morning. While \nguidance has an increasing trend of precipitation, there still is a \nwide range of exactly where it could end up. GFS/Euro models have \ntotal QPF, generally a half an inch to an inch range across the \neastern portions of our CWA along and each of Hwy 83. The Euro \nin particular has totals as high as 2+ inches across the \nSandhills. Conversely, HREF is a bit more bullish with a 30 to \n50 percent chance of a quarter inch or more, east of Hwy 83, \nwith the probability dropping to 10 to 30 percent chance for a \nhalf an inch or more. There is general agreement between models \nthat temperatures should remain above freezing throughout the \nevent, aside from the Euro suggesting a brief period of near to \nslightly below freezing temperatures across western Nebraska \nthat could bring the first dusting of snow for the season. It \nshould be highlighted that there is low confidence in this \nscenario playing out at this time. The potential event will \ncontinue to be monitored closely if there is any increasing \nagreement in intensity, timing, and location of potential \nprecipitation. \n\nAside from the increased shower chances, the upper trough will \nweaken and merge back into the mean flow while a strong upper trough \ndigs into the Midwest from Canada. Daytime highs heading into early \nnext week will return to seasonal norms in the low to mid 60s across \nthe region, with light south southeasterly winds at 10 to 15 \nmph.",
